1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,888
This is part 2 of the E C E 358 midterm review. Part 1, video 26 a, covered Chapter 1, Chapter 2, and reliable transfer up to Selective Repeat.

2
00:00:14,338 --> 00:00:29,442
Part 2 starts with TCP. The instructor says that TCP congestion control is not on this midterm, so it is not here. Then come the exam questions, the formula sheets, and the mistakes to avoid.

3
00:00:29,892 --> 00:00:45,700
This part is TCP, the second half of Chapter 3. Slide 52: TCP is point-to-point, with one sender and one receiver. It gives a reliable, in-order byte stream in both directions: full duplex.

4
00:00:46,150 --> 00:00:53,147
TCP uses cumulative ACKs and pipelining. It is connection-oriented, with flow control.

5
00:00:53,597 --> 00:00:59,784
Slide 53 shows the segment. Each header row is 32 bits, or 4 bytes.

6
00:01:00,234 --> 00:01:05,759
The first row holds the source and destination port numbers. They select the socket.

7
00:01:06,209 --> 00:01:10,689
The sequence number is the number of the first data byte in the segment.

8
00:01:11,139 --> 00:01:17,539
The acknowledgement number is the next byte that the receiver expects. The A bit says that it is valid.

9
00:01:17,989 --> 00:01:25,371
head len counts rows of 4 bytes. With no options it is 5, and 5 times 4 is 20 bytes.

10
00:01:25,821 --> 00:01:31,623
The R, S and F bits are reset, SYN and FIN. They open and close connections.

11
00:01:32,073 --> 00:01:36,191
The receive window field carries rwnd, for flow control.

12
00:01:36,641 --> 00:01:41,099
A common mistake: the sequence number counts bytes, not segments.

13
00:01:41,549 --> 00:01:52,856
For the midterm, you do not need to memorize the header layout. Know what each field means: SYN, ACK, FIN, the sequence number, the ACK number, and the receive window.

14
00:01:53,306 --> 00:01:57,658
Here a segment has sequence number 92 and 8 data bytes.

15
00:01:58,108 --> 00:02:05,447
n is the number of data bytes. The last byte is 92 plus 8 minus 1, which is 99.

16
00:02:05,897 --> 00:02:13,086
The receiver now expects byte 100. So the ACK number is 92 plus 8, which is 100.

17
00:02:13,536 --> 00:02:21,899
The next segments start at 100 and at 120. Their ACKs are 120 and 135.

18
00:02:22,349 --> 00:02:29,517
ACK 135 confirms every byte up to 134. This is a cumulative ACK.

19
00:02:29,967 --> 00:02:39,481
Now suppose that segment 100 is lost. Segment 120 arrives, and bytes 100 to 119 are a gap.

20
00:02:39,931 --> 00:02:47,185
The receiver still expects byte 100. So it sends ACK 100 again. This is a duplicate ACK.

21
00:02:47,635 --> 00:02:56,936
A common mistake is to ACK the last byte received. After bytes 92 to 99, the ACK is 100, not 99.

22
00:02:57,386 --> 00:03:07,242
Slide 56. Host A types one character, C, and host B echoes it. A's next byte is 42. B's next byte is 79.

23
00:03:07,692 --> 00:03:15,308
A sends sequence number 42 with the C. Its ACK, 79, is the next byte that A expects from B.

24
00:03:15,758 --> 00:03:23,779
B echoes the C with its own sequence number, 79. Its ACK is 42 plus 1, which is 43.

25
00:03:24,229 --> 00:03:31,248
A acknowledges the echo: sequence number 43, and ACK 79 plus 1, which is 80.

26
00:03:31,698 --> 00:03:41,575
Each direction has its own sequence numbers. One segment can carry data and an ACK. A reply also swaps the source and destination ports.

27
00:03:42,025 --> 00:03:49,983
A common mistake: B's sequence number is 79, its own byte. It is not 43, the ACK that it got.

28
00:03:50,433 --> 00:04:03,958
Slide 57, the TCP sender. Event 1: data from the application. TCP makes segments 92 and 100 and starts the timer. Segment 100 is lost.

29
00:04:04,408 --> 00:04:13,347
TCP has one retransmission timer per connection, for the oldest unACKed segment. It expires after TimeOutInterval.

30
00:04:13,797 --> 00:04:24,762
Event 2: an ACK for new data. ACK 100 arrives, so SendBase becomes 100. Segment 100 is still unACKed, so the timer starts again.

31
00:04:25,212 --> 00:04:33,191
Event 3: a timeout. A resends only the segment that caused it, Seq 100, and restarts the timer.

32
00:04:33,641 --> 00:04:45,033
A common mistake: TCP has one timer, not one per segment as in Selective Repeat. And it resends one segment, not the window as in Go-Back-N.

33
00:04:45,483 --> 00:04:55,445
Slide 58, the ACK rules of the receiver. Delayed ACKs give fewer small packets. Immediate ACKs give a fast reaction to lost segments.

34
00:04:55,895 --> 00:05:06,925
Rule 1: an in-order segment, and all earlier data is ACKed. Delay the ACK up to 500 ms. If no segment comes, send ACK 100.

35
00:05:07,375 --> 00:05:18,020
Rule 2: an in-order segment while one ACK is pending. Immediately send one cumulative ACK: 100 plus 20, which is 120.

36
00:05:18,470 --> 00:05:27,665
Rule 3: an out-of-order segment, so there is a gap. Immediately send a duplicate ACK 120, the next expected byte.

37
00:05:28,115 --> 00:05:38,205
Rule 4: a segment fills the gap at its lower end. Immediately send ACK 135 plus 10, which is 145.

38
00:05:38,655 --> 00:05:46,079
A common mistake: only rule 1 waits. And the delay must stay short, or the sender's timer expires.

39
00:05:46,529 --> 00:05:54,615
Slides 59 and 60. Case a, lost ACK. A sends Seq 92, and the ACK 100 is lost.

40
00:05:55,065 --> 00:06:03,662
The timer expires, and A resends Seq 92. B sends ACK 100 again. SendBase becomes 100.

41
00:06:04,112 --> 00:06:15,355
Case b, premature timeout. A sends Seq 92 and Seq 100. The ACKs are slow, the timer expires, and A resends Seq 92.

42
00:06:15,805 --> 00:06:27,858
ACK 100 and ACK 120 arrive, so SendBase is 120. The copy of 92 reaches B, and B sends ACK 120 again.

43
00:06:28,308 --> 00:06:34,815
Case c. A sends Seq 92 and Seq 100. The ACK 100 is lost.

44
00:06:35,265 --> 00:06:45,761
ACK 120 arrives before the timeout. So SendBase is 120, there is no resend, and A sends Seq 120.

45
00:06:46,211 --> 00:06:55,299
The rule: a cumulative ACK confirms all earlier bytes. So ACK 120 also covers the lost ACK 100.

46
00:06:55,749 --> 00:07:04,005
A common mistake: the resend in case b is not an error. B drops the copy and repeats ACK 120.

47
00:07:04,455 --> 00:07:15,633
Slide 61, fast retransmit. A sign of a loss lets the sender act before the timeout. Here A sends five segments, and segment 100 is lost.

48
00:07:16,083 --> 00:07:23,593
Segment 92 arrives. B sends ACK 100, the first ACK 100. It is not a duplicate.

49
00:07:24,043 --> 00:07:35,563
Segments 120, 135 and 145 arrive after the gap. Each one gives ACK 100 again: 3 duplicate ACKs.

50
00:07:36,013 --> 00:07:44,994
After 3 duplicate ACKs, A resends the unACKed segment with the smallest sequence number, 100. The timer still runs.

51
00:07:45,444 --> 00:07:57,007
Segment 100 fills the gap. Now B has all bytes before 150, so it sends ACK 145 plus 5, which is 150.

52
00:07:57,457 --> 00:08:06,033
A common mistake is to count the first ACK 100 as a duplicate. You need 3 duplicates, so 4 ACKs in total.

53
00:08:06,483 --> 00:08:15,869
Slides 63 to 68, flow control. The application can read slowly. Then a fast sender overflows the receive buffer.

54
00:08:16,319 --> 00:08:22,549
RcvBuffer is the buffer size. The typical default is 4096 bytes.

55
00:08:22,999 --> 00:08:27,457
Here 1500 bytes are buffered for the application.

56
00:08:27,907 --> 00:08:37,657
The free space is rwnd: 4096 minus 1500, which is 2596 bytes.

57
00:08:38,107 --> 00:08:42,651
The receiver puts rwnd in the receive window field of each segment.

58
00:08:43,101 --> 00:08:50,162
The sender keeps its in-flight, unACKed data at or below rwnd. So the buffer cannot overflow.

59
00:08:50,612 --> 00:09:01,577
A common mistake: rwnd is the free space, 2596 bytes. It is not the buffer size, 4096 bytes.

60
00:09:02,027 --> 00:09:12,929
Slides 69 to 71, the 3-way handshake. It uses the SYN flag, the initial sequence number ISN, the ACK, and the receive window.

61
00:09:13,379 --> 00:09:20,419
The client sends a SYN with a random ISN, 8000. Its RWND is not defined yet.

62
00:09:20,869 --> 00:09:33,669
The server sends SYN plus ACK with its own ISN, 15000. The ACK is 8000 plus 1, which is 8001. RWND 5000 is its buffer.

63
00:09:34,119 --> 00:09:48,391
The client sends an ACK: Seq 8001, ACK 15000 plus 1, which is 15001, and RWND 10000. The client's data starts at byte 8001.

64
00:09:48,841 --> 00:09:56,627
The ISN is random, so data from an old connection between the same end points is not accepted in a new one.

65
00:09:57,077 --> 00:10:05,696
A common mistake: the SYN uses one sequence number. So the ACK is ISN plus 1, although the SYN has no data.

66
00:10:06,146 --> 00:10:15,149
Slide 72. Each side closes its own direction. The client sends FIN bit 1: it has no more data to send.

67
00:10:15,599 --> 00:10:19,780
The server ACKs the FIN. It can still send data to the client.

68
00:10:20,230 --> 00:10:27,931
When the server has no more data, it sends its own FIN. It can also put this FIN in the same segment as the ACK.

69
00:10:28,381 --> 00:10:35,891
The client ACKs the server's FIN. Both directions are closed. Simultaneous FINs are also handled.

70
00:10:36,341 --> 00:10:40,437
A common mistake: one FIN closes only one direction.

71
00:10:40,887 --> 00:10:50,935
Now the exam. The instructor gave its structure: five questions, each with many parts. A dot marks the chapters that each question uses.

72
00:10:51,385 --> 00:10:59,662
Question 1: twenty multiple-choice parts, half a mark each. Some need a calculation. It covers all three chapters.

73
00:11:00,112 --> 00:11:07,600
Question 2: ten parts, all from the question bank. The full videos 1 to 10 solve the bank questions.

74
00:11:08,050 --> 00:11:15,709
Question 3: many calculations, mainly from Chapter 1. Videos 1 to 4 and 26 a teach them.

75
00:11:16,159 --> 00:11:24,329
Question 4: ARQ protocols, with concepts and calculations. Videos 8 and 9, and 26 a.

76
00:11:24,779 --> 00:11:31,265
Question 5: TCP, without congestion control. Video 10, and 26 b.

77
00:11:31,715 --> 00:11:44,408
Out of scope: TCP congestion control, and probability. You need not memorize header layouts, but know what each field means, for example SYN, ACK, and the sequence number.

78
00:11:44,858 --> 00:11:53,391
Next: one new practice example for each question. Then the lists of metrics and parameters, the common mistakes and a recap.

79
00:11:53,841 --> 00:12:04,380
Practice for questions 1 and 2, short answers. TCP or UDP for four new applications? Give the reason. Pause the video and try it.

80
00:12:04,830 --> 00:12:10,931
First ask: must every byte arrive? Then use TCP. It ACKs and retransmits.

81
00:12:11,381 --> 00:12:18,165
If not, ask: does the application need low delay, and can it lose some data? Then use UDP.

82
00:12:18,615 --> 00:12:27,661
One. A DNS query uses UDP, port 53. It is one short query and one reply, with no connection setup.

83
00:12:28,111 --> 00:12:34,937
Two. An interactive game needs low delay. A retransmission comes too late. So, UDP.

84
00:12:35,387 --> 00:12:41,489
Three. A web transaction needs 100 percent reliable data. So, TCP.

85
00:12:41,939 --> 00:12:47,101
Four. Audio tolerates some loss and needs its rate. So, UDP.

86
00:12:47,551 --> 00:12:58,047
Answer in three parts: the protocol, the need, and the service that meets it. For ports, HTTP and Go-Back-N, see video 25 b.

87
00:12:58,497 --> 00:13:11,297
Practice for exam question 3, Chapter 1 calculations. 2 megabits cross 2 links of 1 megabit per second, through a store-and-forward switch. Pause the video and try it.

88
00:13:11,747 --> 00:13:18,915
Part a. L over R: 2 times 10 to the 6, over 10 to the 6. That is 2 seconds on link 1.

89
00:13:19,365 --> 00:13:28,027
The switch sends only after the whole message arrives. Link 2 runs from 2 to 4 seconds. The total is 4 seconds.

90
00:13:28,477 --> 00:13:37,906
Part b. One packet: 10 to the 4 over 10 to the 6, so 10 milliseconds per link. Packet 1 arrives at 20 milliseconds.

91
00:13:38,356 --> 00:13:50,921
Then both links work in parallel: one packet every 10 milliseconds. The last one arrives at 20 plus 199 times 10, which is 2010 milliseconds.

92
00:13:51,371 --> 00:14:04,513
The rule: links plus packets minus 1, so 201 times 10. 2.01 seconds is about half of 4. With the 3 links of the handout, it was about one third.

93
00:14:04,963 --> 00:14:18,339
More practice for exam question 3. A sends 500 kilobytes to B in packets of 1 kilobyte. The links run at 1, 2 and 2 megabits per second. Pause the video and try it.

94
00:14:18,789 --> 00:14:23,503
One packet: 1000 bytes times 8, so 8000 bits.

95
00:14:23,953 --> 00:14:29,649
Link 1 needs 8 milliseconds per packet. Links 2 and 3 need 4 milliseconds.

96
00:14:30,099 --> 00:14:37,054
Packet 1 crosses the links one after the other: 8 plus 4 plus 4, so 16 milliseconds.

97
00:14:37,504 --> 00:14:45,909
Links 2 and 3 are faster, so they wait for link 1. B gets one packet every 8 milliseconds, and no queue forms.

98
00:14:46,359 --> 00:14:56,215
There are 500 packets. The last one arrives at 16 plus 499 times 8, which is 4008 milliseconds.

99
00:14:56,665 --> 00:15:06,585
The throughput: 4 times 10 to the 6 bits over 4.008 seconds, about 998 kilobits per second.

100
00:15:07,035 --> 00:15:18,001
The bottleneck estimate uses the slowest link, 1 megabit per second. 4 times 10 to the 6 bits take 4 seconds, only 8 milliseconds less.

101
00:15:18,451 --> 00:15:35,944
Practice for exam question 4, ARQ calculations. Frames go from A through B to C. Link A B uses a window, and link B C uses stop-and-wait. Find the smallest rate of B C that does not flood B. Pause the video and try it.

102
00:15:36,394 --> 00:15:46,101
Link A B. The propagation delay is 300 kilometers over 2 times 10 to the 8 meters per second: 1.5 milliseconds.

103
00:15:46,551 --> 00:15:52,588
The transmission delay is 2000 bits over 2 megabits per second: 1 millisecond.

104
00:15:53,038 --> 00:15:59,566
The ACK of frame 1 is back after 1 plus 2 times 1.5, which is 4 milliseconds.

105
00:16:00,016 --> 00:16:09,616
In these 4 milliseconds, A sends 3 frames and is busy for 3. The utilization is 3 over 4, which is 0.75.

106
00:16:10,066 --> 00:16:21,607
So B receives 0.75 times 2 megabits per second. That is 1.5 megabits per second, or 750 frames per second.

107
00:16:22,057 --> 00:16:33,897
Link B C. Its propagation delay is 0.25 milliseconds. One stop-and-wait cycle is the transmission delay plus 0.5 milliseconds.

108
00:16:34,347 --> 00:16:43,755
B is not flooded when its output is at least its input. So 1 over T B C must be at least 750 frames per second.

109
00:16:44,205 --> 00:16:54,680
So one cycle may last 4 thirds of a millisecond. Take away 0.5. The transmission delay is at most 5 sixths of a millisecond.

110
00:16:55,130 --> 00:17:02,490
So the rate is at least 2000 bits over 5 sixths of a millisecond: 2.4 megabits per second.

111
00:17:02,940 --> 00:17:14,865
Part b. A window of 5 gives 5 over 4, which is 1.25. The utilization stops at 1, so B receives 1000 frames per second.

112
00:17:15,315 --> 00:17:26,174
Now a cycle may last 1 millisecond. The transmission delay is at most 0.5 milliseconds. So the rate is at least 4 megabits per second.

113
00:17:26,624 --> 00:17:44,565
Even a very fast link B C sends at most 1 over 0.5 milliseconds, which is 2000 frames per second. That is more than 1000, so a rate exists. In the handout, the limit was 500, so B was always flooded.

114
00:17:45,015 --> 00:17:56,343
More practice for exam question 4: Selective Repeat with a 3-bit sequence number field, and two windows of equal size W. Pause the video and try it.

115
00:17:56,793 --> 00:18:03,065
Part a. 3 bits give 2 to the 3, which is 8 numbers: 0 to 7. So K is 8.

116
00:18:03,515 --> 00:18:10,491
Part b. Take W equal to 5. The sender sends 0 to 4, and it may have to resend them.

117
00:18:10,941 --> 00:18:17,085
The receiver gets all five. Its window moves on to 5, 6, 7, 0 and 1.

118
00:18:17,535 --> 00:18:23,551
All five ACKs are lost. After the timeout, the sender resends the old packet 0.

119
00:18:24,001 --> 00:18:30,401
0 is in the receiver window. So the old copy is accepted as new data: a duplicate.

120
00:18:30,851 --> 00:18:41,945
Part c. No number may be in both groups, so W s plus W r is at most K. 2 W is at most 8, so W is at most 4.

121
00:18:42,395 --> 00:18:51,589
Check W equal to 4. The sender may resend 0 to 3, and the receiver accepts 4 to 7. No number is in both.

122
00:18:52,039 --> 00:19:13,309
Practice for exam question 5, TCP. B has all bytes up to 499. A sends segments of 100, 60 and 200 bytes. Segment 1 has sequence number 500. A uses port 4021, and B uses port 80. Pause the video and try it.

123
00:19:13,759 --> 00:19:20,713
A sequence number is the first byte. Segment 1 carries bytes 500 to 599.

124
00:19:21,163 --> 00:19:26,027
Segment 2 starts at 500 plus 100, which is 600.

125
00:19:26,477 --> 00:19:34,968
Segment 3 starts at 600 plus 60, which is 660. It ends at byte 859.

126
00:19:35,418 --> 00:19:43,077
Part b. An ACK number is the next byte expected: 600, 660 and 860.

127
00:19:43,527 --> 00:19:50,439
The ACKs go from B to A, so the ports swap: source 80, destination 4021.

128
00:19:50,889 --> 00:19:54,238
Part c. Segment 1 arrives: ACK 600.

129
00:19:54,688 --> 00:19:56,309
Segment 2 is lost.

130
00:19:56,759 --> 00:20:05,527
Segment 3 arrives out of order. B buffers it and sends a duplicate ACK 600. It still waits for byte 600.

131
00:20:05,977 --> 00:20:13,657
The timer of segment 2 expires, and A resends it. The gap is filled: a cumulative ACK 860.

132
00:20:14,107 --> 00:20:19,099
One duplicate ACK does not start a fast retransmit. That needs 3.

133
00:20:19,549 --> 00:20:34,141
Now the lists that the instructor asked for. First, every metric and how to compute it. Chapter 1. The nodal delay is the sum of four delays. d trans is L over R, and d prop is d over s.

134
00:20:34,591 --> 00:20:41,845
The traffic intensity is L a over R. Near 1, the queues get long. Above 1, they grow without limit.

135
00:20:42,295 --> 00:20:51,596
End to end, store-and-forward: links plus packets minus 1, times L over R. The throughput is the rate of the slowest link.

136
00:20:52,046 --> 00:20:58,979
Circuit switching: the link rate over the rate of one user. The bit error rate: bit errors over bits sent.

137
00:20:59,429 --> 00:21:16,645
Chapter 2 and 3 metrics. The RTT is the time for a small packet to go there and back. On one link with short ACKs, it is 2 d prop. Non-persistent HTTP needs 2 RTTs plus F over T x per object.

138
00:21:17,095 --> 00:21:28,082
With a base file and N objects, non-persistent HTTP repeats this for every file. Persistent HTTP opens the connection once.

139
00:21:28,532 --> 00:21:33,353
A DNS lookup adds one RTT for each DNS server.

140
00:21:33,803 --> 00:21:45,153
The utilization of stop-and-wait is L over R, over RTT plus L over R. A window of W multiplies it by W, up to 1.

141
00:21:45,603 --> 00:21:55,203
The throughput is U times R. Divide by L for frames per second. The average throughput of a file is its size over the total time.

142
00:21:55,653 --> 00:22:09,221
Now the parameters. The ideal window is RTT plus L over R, over L over R. In Selective Repeat, W is at most K over 2, with K equal to 2 to the k.

143
00:22:09,671 --> 00:22:21,468
Go-Back-N uses cumulative ACKs and one timer. Selective Repeat ACKs and times each packet. The checksum, the sequence numbers and the timer fix the four errors.

144
00:22:21,918 --> 00:22:33,907
TCP. The sequence number is the first data byte. The ACK number is the next byte expected. A gap gives a duplicate ACK, and three of them start a fast retransmit.

145
00:22:34,357 --> 00:22:45,600
Know what each header field means, not the layout. Ports pick the process. SYN and FIN open and close. R wind is the free buffer space, for flow control.

146
00:22:46,050 --> 00:22:54,711
Last, the common mistakes. Rates are in bits: 1000 bytes are 8000 bits, so 8 milliseconds, not 1.

147
00:22:55,161 --> 00:23:01,497
Store-and-forward links work in parallel: 2010 milliseconds, not 4000.

148
00:23:01,947 --> 00:23:07,387
The ACK needs a second propagation delay: 4 milliseconds, not 2.5.

149
00:23:07,837 --> 00:23:12,232
The utilization stops at 1: 1.25 becomes 1.

150
00:23:12,682 --> 00:23:18,783
K counts numbers, not bits. 3 bits give 8 numbers, so W is at most 4.

151
00:23:19,233 --> 00:23:26,508
The ACK number is the next byte expected: 600, not 599. And the ports swap.

152
00:23:26,958 --> 00:23:31,971
A missing segment repeats the ACK: 600, not 860.

153
00:23:32,421 --> 00:23:42,448
Non-persistent HTTP needs 2 RTTs per object, not 1. Do not forget the RTT that opens TCP.

154
00:23:42,898 --> 00:23:48,957
Recap. For multiple choice and the question bank, give the protocol, the need and the service.

155
00:23:49,407 --> 00:23:58,409
For Chapter 1 calculations, draw a row per link. After packet 1, one packet arrives per packet time of the slowest link.

156
00:23:58,859 --> 00:24:10,123
For ARQ, the input is U times R, with U at most 1. The output must be at least the input. In Selective Repeat, W is at most K over 2.

157
00:24:10,573 --> 00:24:15,672
For TCP: the first byte, the next byte expected, and the ports swap.

158
00:24:16,122 --> 00:24:24,037
The instructor's advice: redo every slide example on paper, solve the question bank, and review the tutorials.

159
00:24:24,487 --> 00:24:31,889
Use the lists of metrics and parameters, and check the mistakes. That is the end of the midterm video. Good luck.
